(A Vox compiler discrepancy found while building scripts/repin-citations
 on 2026-08-22 — not a ledger row, recorded here because PROCEDURE.md
 section 5 says record, do not adjudicate.

 The claim it contradicted: LANGUAGE.md:780 — "Variables declared at top
 level are global and can be used inside functions." Not in the 0.4.10
 register (#66-#91) — re-verified unchanged against 0.4.10, 2026-08-22:
 byte-identical diagnostic, still reproduced. (Not the same bug as
 functions.md Discrepancy 1 / vox #66, which was about a global's TYPE
 being read wrong from a forward-referencing function — that one is
 fixed. This was a distinct refusal, "Unknown variable", triggered by
 top-level `Set` + a function read, on collection types only.)

 RESOLVED 2026-08-25 — vox bug #92, fixed 0.4.13: a top-level `Set` on a
 global `list` (or `map`) that a function reads is now legal — the
 binding stays in scope, and the function sees the new value. The program
 below compiles and prints "1", exactly what LANGUAGE.md:780 promises
 ("variables declared at top level are global and can be used inside
 functions"). Re-recorded to the current behavior: the probe now asserts
 the value the manual promises instead of the old refusal.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=../vox/coreasm ../vox/target/release/vox docs/ledger/probes/repin/vox-global-list-set.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   1
)
a list called xs is [].

To 'how many'.
    Return a number, xs's length.

Set xs to ["a"].
Print 'how many'.
